### Historical source correction

The Oklahoma item is accessible: [J. Albert's course project list, Version 3, Summer 2008](https://www.math.ou.edu/~jalbert/courses/projects.pdf), item 15. Its exact characterization is “least-energy configurations of discs (I think) on a cone,” and it points to the 2005/2007 experiments. It is an informal course-project suggestion, not a formal published densest-packing conjecture or proof. The book's summary should not be upgraded into a precise theorem statement.

### Search and access limitations

Primary texts were preferred over generated search answers. Search covered the book's titles, cone hard-disk packing, conical crystal ground states, phyllotaxis chirality, Fermat-spiral density, and 2025–2026 developments. Multiple publisher pages returned 403/401; author manuscripts, deposited abstracts, or PubMed were used with the reading level stated above. One search-suggested `preprints.org/manuscript/202608.2110` URL was inaccessible and not independently located by its identifier; it supports no claim. Absence from this bounded review establishes neither nonexistence nor novelty.

## 5. Research direction after these certificates

The attainable result is not “Fibonacci is always optimal,” but a precise separation between geometric constraints, kinetic branch selection, and equilibrium. The full target needs a physically grounded energy and a broader contact-topology class. Fixing those assumptions is the single highest-value prerequisite; more random starts in an arbitrary energy landscape would not repair their absence.

