     1	# Status and strongest comparable results
     2	
     3	Snapshot: 2026-10-14 (America/Los_Angeles). This is a bounded primary-source review, not a proof that no relevant result exists.
     4	
     5	## 1. The actual question and current verdict
     6	
     7	The book asks two different things:
     8	
     9	- **C1:** whether the observed Fibonacci parastichy patterns are least-*elastic*-energy configurations of identical units on a cone; it also discusses an equal-disk-packing version.
    10	- **C2:** whether varying disk size in packing toward the apex alternates which counter-rotating family has the larger consecutive-Fibonacci count.
    11	
    12	**Overall verdict here: PARTIAL.** We found no verified general theorem resolving these statements under the book's unspecified elastic law and growth conditions. This is **not** a certified literature-wide “still unsolved” claim. The book itself calls both questions conjectural; the retrieved modern sources concern different precise models and do not announce a general solution of Cao's conjecture.
    13	
    14	The central modern distinction is explicit in Lee and Levitov [3], Section II.1: “the structure does not track the global energy minimum.” The same passage says the Fibonacci structures characterize a progression of deformation, “rather than the result of global energy minimization.” Consequently, citing that theory as a global-minimum proof would reverse its stated qualification.
    15	
    16	**What this folder settles:** a conventional fixed-golden-angle, equal-area spiral loses to a triangular-lattice quotient in a certified 183-center annular comparison, and in an asymptotic density comparison after any finite apex deletion. This does not classify the much larger family of deformed Fibonacci-contact patterns. Reflection degeneracy is proved for achiral models; alternating relative chirality is proved only for an explicitly defined local Fibonacci-family model.
    17	
    18	**Difficulty assessment:** the finite witness and reduced-model algebra are accessible because the cone has a 60-degree commensurate development, distances reduce to an integer quadratic form, and a single short pair suffices to disprove an ansatz. Those are the actual bounded obligations. The full experimental elastic minimization is not shown to be “not too hard”: it requires a constitutive functional, stress and boundary data, a global competitor class, and a proof excluding all lower-energy competitors. No ease claim follows from having a numerical simulator.
