   208	
   209	The finite verifier uses only Python integer arithmetic and `fractions.Fraction`; floating values are printed after certification and never decide acceptance. Its negative controls reject a repeated orbit, an apex point outside the allowed annulus, and diameter `101/100`. Generator and verifier share the mathematical lattice definition, but use different coordinate representations and share no implementation functions. The mpmath check uses a stable sine-squared polar distance formula rather than the verifier's cosine lower bound. Both codes run under the same Python runtime; they are not formal kernel verification.
   210	
   211	## 7. Independent review
   212	
   213	The hostile-referee prompt and its response are kept in `review/`. Review completion and point-by-point dispositions are recorded here after the independent run. The proofs and evidence are not considered externally certified merely because a model agrees.
   214	
   215	## 8. Exact remaining blocker and next action
   216	
   217	Nothing here establishes that the actual Ag/silica experiments reach global elastic minima, or that every Fibonacci-contact packing is inferior. A full answer requires the strain/bending/substrate energy, geometry and stress history, defect costs, boundaries, and allowed rearrangements. A scalar Riesz energy or intrinsic hard-disk model is not automatically that functional. No sequential packing experiment has been substituted for a proof of C2.
   218	
   219	The single most valuable next action is to obtain or derive the experimental bilayer elastic functional and a mathematically explicit Fibonacci-contact competitor class, then certify an energy comparison with a seam-bearing crystal at an experimental cone angle. Until that contract is fixed, more unconstrained numerical searches would answer a different question rather than close this blocker.

